{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>וְעָשְׂתָה אֶת צִפָּרְנֶיהָ</b> (and she shall let her nails grow) — she shall grow them out so that she becomes repulsive, in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 21:12), according to the words of Rabbi Akiva (Yevamot 48a), and so Onkelos explained (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 21:12). Now according to their opinion, <b>וְעָשְׂתָה</b> will be like וְעָשָׂת אֶת הַתְּבוּאָה (Leviticus 25:21), for growth is called עֲשִׂיָּה (making). But in the Sifrei (Sifrei Tetzeh 7) they cited a proof for the words of Rabbi Eliezer: וְלֹא עָשָׂה רַגְלָיו וְלֹא עָשָׂה שְׂפָמוֹ (2 Samuel 19:25), and this is a great proof. Therefore I say that all of these are rites of mourning, and everything follows from וּבָכְתָה אֶת אָבִיהָ וְאֶת אִמָּהּ (Deuteronomy 21:13), and He commanded that she shave her head as is the way of mourners, as in וַיָּגָז אֶת רֹאשׁוֹ (Job 1:20) — Job, and likewise גָּזִּי נִזְרֵךְ (Jeremiah 7:29), and likewise the cutting of the nails is mourning like the shaving of the head. And He said וְהֵסִירָה אֶת שִׂמְלַת שִׁבְיָהּ מֵעָלֶיהָ, meaning she shall don garments of mourning, and she shall sit in your house like a widow and not go outside at all, and she shall weep for her father and her mother, and she shall do all this for a month of days, for so is the way of mourners.\n\nAnd according to the opinion of our Sages (Sifrei Tetzeh 11) who say that all of it is to make her ugly, He commanded that she remove the beautiful garments from upon her, for the accursed nations — their daughters adorn themselves in battle in order to commit harlotry after them, and that she shave her head, which is a great disfigurement, and that she cut her nails, for it is the custom of women to grow them and dye them with kinds of kohl and pigments, and Scripture calls the cutting עֲשִׂיָּה, because the hair of the legs and the beard — also the nails, to grow them one cuts them. And what seems right in my eyes is that it is an elliptical expression: they were accustomed to abbreviate in what is understood, and the meaning of לֹא עָשָׂה אֶת רַגְלָיו וְאֶת שְׂפָמוֹ — the act done to them — alludes to shaving the hair of the legs and the beard, or to washing the feet themselves, and so too is the opinion of Jonathan ben Uzziel, who said: לֹא שָׁטַף יָת רַגְלֵיהּ וְלָא סַפַּר שְׂפָמֵהּ (he did not wash his feet and did not trim his mustache), and likewise וְעָשְׂתָה אֶת צִפָּרְנֶיהָ — the act of the nails. And the commentators (see Rashi Genesis 1:7) said that the meaning of עֲשִׂיָּה is תִּקּוּן (preparation/adornment), as in וַיְמַהֵר לַעֲשׂוֹת אוֹתוֹ (ibid. 18:7).\n\nNow the reason in this passage is that she converts under coercion, and she is not asked whether she desires to abandon her faith and become Jewish as is done with other converts; rather, the husband tells her that she must keep the Torah of Israel under coercion and abandon her worship. And this is the meaning of וּבָכְתָה אֶת אָבִיהָ וְאֶת אִמָּהּ — that she is leaving her people and her Elohim. And this is the midrash of Rabbi Akiva: her father means only idolatry, as in what is said (Jeremiah 2:27), they say to the wood, you are my father, and to the stone, you gave birth to me. And the upshot is that she mourns over abandoning her faith and becoming part of another people. And it is possible that a court immerses her under coercion, as is done with slaves. And since she does not become Jewish according to the proper procedure, Scripture distanced her all this time. And the reason for the mourning and the weeping according to our Sages is so that she become repulsive — perhaps his desire for her will pass. And Rabbi Eliezer said that the Torah gave her a period like the law of those who mourn the dead, in honor of her father and mother who died in battle.\n\nAnd the Rav said in the Guide of the Perplexed (III:41), out of compassion for her, so that she find rest for her soul, for those who grieve find repose and calm in their weeping and mourning, and during that time he shall not compel her to abandon her faith nor consummate with her. But in my opinion, the purpose is not compassion for her, but rather to uproot the name of idolatry from her mouth and from her heart, and to extinguish the still-glowing ember of bereavement and separation from her father and mother and her people, for it is not fitting to lie with a woman who is coerced and grieving, as our Sages, of blessed memory, mentioned (Nedarim 20b) regarding mamzerim — children of coerced unions (lit. divorcees of the heart) — and all the more so this one who cries out in her heart to her Elohim to save her and restore her to her people and to her Elohim. Now when they inform her that they will compel her to abandon her people and her birthplace and become Jewish, they say to her: be comforted over your father and your mother and over the land of your birth, for you shall not see them again forever; rather, you shall be a wife to your master according to the law of Moses and Judaism. And then she is given time to weep and mourn according to the custom of mourners, to calm her distress and longing, for in every grief there will afterward be relief and consolation. And during this time she takes counsel with her soul regarding conversion, and some of the idolatry of her people and her birthplace is uprooted from her heart, for she is consoled over them and cleaves to this man whom she knows she shall be joined to, and she becomes accustomed to him. Therefore Scripture said וַהֲבֵאתָהּ אֶל תּוֹךְ בֵּיתֶךָ, and they interpreted (Sifrei 7): your house and not another house, and likewise וְיָשְׁבָה בְּבֵיתֶךָ — that she shall sit all this time in the house that he uses (ibid. 9), perhaps she will desire and be satisfied with him.\n\nAnd on the general principle that all of this is because of the coercion — but if she wanted to convert willingly in a court according to the law, she is permitted immediately, to him, to his father, and to his brother. And so they said in the chapter HaCholetz (Yevamot 47b): וּבָכְתָה אֶת אָבִיהָ וְאֶת אִמָּהּ יֶרַח יָמִים — in what case is this said? When she did not accept upon herself; but if she accepted upon herself, they immerse her and she is permitted immediately. And it is possible that in all the captives taken in battle it was done according to this Torah, for out of fear they would agree to convert.\n\nAnd He said (Deuteronomy 21:14) וְשִׁלַּחְתָּהּ לְנַפְשָׁהּ — she shall do as she wishes, and we shall not compel her to keep the law of Moses and Judaism, for one who converts willingly — we compel her to keep the Torah, and if she profanes Shabbat she is lashed, and if she eats pork she is punished as a lawless Israelite who apostatizes. But this one too — if she acknowledged conversion with her mouth without coercion, we do not send her away to her own devices, for even if we suppose that her conversion was out of fear, her law is as a full Israelite, for we have already noted (Yevamot 24a): the halakhah — all of them are converts."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ור\"א (before אָמַר) resolved as Rabbi Eliezer, following the context where his opinion is cited from the Sifrei; the later ור\"ל is not present in this passage. The phrase בני גרושת הלב is rendered literally as 'children of coerced unions (lit. divorcees of the heart).'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:53:37.818822066Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}